A: The issue here is that PexRunner is trying to generate values for a parameter (ArrayList and int) that it cannot provide. The fact is that the generic types of System.Collections.ArrayList, which under the hood is an ArrayList in .NET, are tricky to deal with, given the potential range of values.
The PexRunner has difficulty figuring out what to generate for those types, since ArrayList could potentially contain an endless range of valid data.
One way to deal with this (although not perfect) is to test this method with a more specific type. For instance, consider testing with List<int> instead of ArrayList.
I have a strong suspicion that a better solution would be to refactor this situation so that it doesn't require an ArrayList. .NET provides a much more straightforward construct for working with lists of objects, the List<T> class. If you could do that, I would be perfectly happy to help again.
In any case, I hope this is still useful to you.
Best
Igor 

  [1] I'm sorry, I don't see any code that was shared. To provide you with a better response, could you please paste the code snippet you are referring to? I can analyze the code snippet to provide a more appropriate solution for you.
I have addressed this by refactoring the problem. I have replaced the ArrayList type with the List<T> generic type. This allows PexRunner to generate reasonable test data for the method under test.
The new code looks like this:
